        <li>Wikipedia. "Historical mortality rates of puerperal fever." (The monthly and yearly tables Semmelweis published in 1861; First Clinic 1846 with 4,010 deliveries and 459 deaths, 11.4 percent, against the Second Clinic's 3,754 deliveries and 105 deaths, 2.8 percent; monthly highs including 22.6 percent in November 1841, 29.3 percent in October 1842 and 31.4 percent in December 1842; the April 1847 figure of 312 deliveries and 57 deaths; the hand washing policy instituted mid-May 1847; 1848 with 3,556 deliveries and 45 deaths, 1.3 percent; two months with no deaths; his dismissal in March 1849; the rise in the First Clinic to 4.0 percent in 1852 and 9.1 percent in 1854; the fall in maternal deaths after the ward's second clinic was handed to midwives only in 1841; the separation of the two training groups in 1841; yearly rates of about 0.5 to 1 percent before the advent of pathological anatomy in Vienna and 5 to 9 percent after; the comparison with the Dublin maternity hospital at about 1 percent through the same decades, because Dublin's maternity hospital stood apart from a general hospital and its students did not perform autopsies; his own explanation of the winter peaks through the students' autopsy schedule; his return to the assistant's post on 20 March 1847 after a morning in the morgue with Rokitansky's permission; and the quote "Only God knows the number of patients who went prematurely to their graves because of me.")</li>
        <li>Wikipedia. "Contemporary reaction to Ignaz Semmelweis." (His key claim of cadaveric particles; that ordinary soap washings did not remove the particles because the stench stayed on the hands for days; his conclusion that the disease was iatrogenic, or caused by doctors; Scanzoni taking personal offence and remaining one of his most ardent critics; the criticism that he had said nothing new; the case of the discharging uterine cancer over which eleven of twelve women died after examiners washed with soap only; the case of the discharging knee that led him to argue that airborne decaying matter also caused the disease; and the modern explanation, that chlorinated lime destroys bacteria and not merely odour, that most severe puerperal fever is streptococcal, and that disinfecting only after visits to the morgue is not enough.)</li>
        <li>Science History Institute. "Ignaz Semmelweis," by Róza Gombos, September 2025. (His attempt to work as an internist under Joseph Skoda and the post going to another candidate; the shift of view from miasma to a specific preventable cause; his testing of hand washing with soap, water and a nail brush after autopsies before moving to chlorinated water and then chlorinated lime, which was also used to whiten paper, clean stables and sterilise drinking water; the removal of odour and with it the assumed poison; the drop from 18.27 percent to 1.27 percent; Johann Klein's failure to grasp the significance; Karl Haller's help in spreading word of the cadaverous poison; Klein's dismissal of Semmelweis on 20 March 1849; work at Szent Rókus hospital in Pest and the 1861 book; the hostile reception and the open letters, including the passage about thousands upon thousands of mothers and infants who perished and about standing guard; the toll on his mental health; and the line that his goal was not academic recognition but that the child could keep their mother.)</li>
        <li>Schreiner, S. "Ignaz Semmelweis: a victim of harassment?" <em>Wiener Medizinische Wochenschrift</em>, 2020. (His philosophical studies and botanical Latin doctoral thesis of 1844; the conviction in later life that he had no talent as a writer, and the resulting fourteen year delay before publishing his book; the 1861 publication of <em>Die Aetiologie, der Begriff und die Prophylaxis des Kindbettfiebers</em> and the open letters of 1861 and 1862; the failure of the scientific community to accept the book; the journey to Vienna in 1865 under the impression that he was going to rest; admission to the newly built Niederösterreichische Landesirrenanstalt; his attempt to escape on realising what was happening; and his death from sepsis caused by open wounds and a dirty straitjacket two weeks later.)</li>
        <li>British Journal of Anaesthesia / PMC. "Semmelweis and the aetiology of puerperal sepsis 160 years on." (Jakob Kolletschka, Professor of Forensic Medicine, died of disseminated sepsis in March 1847 from a scalpel wound received during an autopsy, and higher mortality in both Vienna clinics than at the Dublin Maternity Hospital over the same period.)</li>
        <li>Harvard Medicine, "They All Laughed," and PMC, "Oliver Wendell Holmes (1809-1894) and his essay on puerperal fever." (Holmes read "The Contagiousness of Puerperal Fever" before the Boston Society for Medical Improvement on 13 February 1843; the essay was rejected and he was denounced for it; his findings were made independently of Semmelweis; and the later account of the hostility it drew.)</li>
        <li>Pennsylvania State University thesis on the reception of puerperal fever contagion in antebellum America. (Hugh Lenox Hodge and Charles Delucena Meigs of Philadelphia as the leading opponents of Holmes's argument that a physician could transmit the disease.)</li>
        <li>World Health Organization. "Maternal mortality," fact sheet, 7 April 2025. (About 260,000 maternal deaths in 2023, over 700 a day and one almost every two minutes; a 40 percent fall in the global maternal mortality ratio between 2000 and 2023, from 328 to 197 deaths per 100,000 live births; infection after childbirth among the major complications accounting for around 75 percent of maternal deaths; and that infection after childbirth can be eliminated where good hygiene is practised and early signs are treated.)</li>
        <li>World Health Organization. "World Hand Hygiene Day 2021: key facts and figures." (Appropriate hand hygiene prevents up to 50 percent of avoidable infections acquired during health care delivery; 7 of every 100 patients in developed countries and 15 of every 100 in developing countries acquire at least one health care associated infection in acute care hospitals; hand hygiene compliance around 9 percent during care of critically ill patients in low income countries and rarely above 70 percent in high income countries; 1 in 3 facilities lacking hand hygiene facilities at the point of care; and about 1 million of the 4.1 million maternal and neonatal deaths each year possibly related to unhygienic birthing practices including lack of hand hygiene.)</li>
